3c0d2aaf339b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which finite-set inclusion satisfies the subset condition?","options":["$\\{1,2\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$","$\\{1,4\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$","$\\{3,4\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$","$\\{2,5\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8418c4f8afa9234e8bc992eb"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is a subset of every set?","options":["$\\varnothing$","$\\{0\\}$","$\\mathbb{R}$","$\\{1\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"54454bcc4236b440ad73cf14"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is true about subsets?","options":["No set is a subset of itself.","Every set is a subset of itself.","Only finite sets can be subsets.","A subset must always be a proper subset."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"35bcc5859726ae9553f3131d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does transitivity of subset relations mean?","options":["If $A\\subseteq B$, then $B\\subseteq A$.","If $A\\subseteq B$, then $A = B$.","If $A\\subseteq B$, then $C\\subseteq A$ for every set $C$.","If $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq C$, then $A\\subseteq C$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e29ec2f445491362427cef9f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A = \\{1\\}$, $B = \\{1,2\\}$, and $C = \\{1,2,3\\}$. Which conclusion follows from transitivity?","options":["$C\\subseteq A$","$B\\subseteq A$","$A\\subseteq C$","$C\\subseteq B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91d4dfc6962acc53549b5e6a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Is $\\{2,3\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,3,4\\}$?","options":["Yes, because both $2$ and $3$ are elements of $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.","No, because $1$ is not in $\\{2,3\\}$.","No, because the larger set has more elements.","Yes, only because the empty set is involved."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0b66abfcfe9c2ffeb8474a8c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does a proper subset differ from the relation $\\subseteq$ as described?","options":["A proper subset excludes equality.","A proper subset must be equal to the larger set.","A proper subset contains every possible object.","A proper subset is never contained in another set."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7f751bdd8d7fc9a85ff8518d"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement avoids a common mistake about $A\\subseteq B$?","options":["$A\\subseteq B$ always requires $A = B$.","$A\\subseteq B$ does not require $B\\subseteq A$.","$A\\subseteq B$ always requires $A$ to be empty.","$A\\subseteq B$ means every element of $B$ lies outside $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a55f3bb7d292aee74bade8c4"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A = \\{1,2\\}$ and $B = \\{1,2\\}$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$A$ is a proper subset of $B$ because all elements match.","$A\\nsubseteq B$ because the sets are equal.","$A\\subseteq B$, but $A$ is not a proper subset of $B$.","$B\\nsubseteq A$ because the sets are equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"942c6834ee9d3770f76b363f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_subset_b056f914_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $A\\subseteq B$ and an object $x$ is known to satisfy $x\\in A$. What must be true?","options":["$x\\notin B$","$B\\subseteq A$","$A = B$","$x\\in B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2cd5be35f896029f69b7a72e"},{"id":"subset_new_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\{1,2,3\\}$, which is the correct relationship of the object $1$ to the set?","options":["$1\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; $1$ is an element, not a subset","$1\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$","both $1\\in$ and $1\\subseteq$ hold","neither relationship holds"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5986d49afe46b73594b2a5e3"},{"id":"subset_new_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is $\\varnothing\\subseteq\\varnothing$?","options":["Yes; vacuous: $\\varnothing$ has no elements","No; the empty set has no subsets","Only sets with elements have subsets","It is undefined"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"41aa26113454c218320f255e"},{"id":"subset_new_015","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Does $A\\subseteq B$ mean the same as $A\\in B$?","options":["No; these are different set relations.","Yes, they are interchangeable","$A\\in B$ always implies $A\\subseteq B$","$A\\subseteq B$ always implies $A\\in B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9c4fc8d9fe59702eeca3713b"},{"id":"subset_new_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How do you correctly express that the element $1$ forms a subset of $\\{1,2\\}$?","options":["$\\{1\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2\\}$","$1\\subseteq\\{1,2\\}$","$\\{1\\}\\in\\{1,2\\}$","$1\\subseteq 1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a1851a57a733cf7c97e83547"},{"id":"subset_new_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\{0,1\\}$. Which relation is always true?","options":["$A\\subseteq\\varnothing$","$A\\subseteq A$","$\\{2\\}\\subseteq A$","$A\\subseteq\\{0\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"99da8b27777a92d3a4d584ae"},{"id":"subset_new_018","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"How many subsets does $\\{a,b\\}$ have?","options":["$4$","$2$","$3$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76a25e7440e003d8dc67f0c4"},{"id":"subset_new_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What follows if $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$?","options":["$A=B$","$A$ and $B$ are disjoint.","$A$ is empty.","$B$ is a proper subset of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3168e7ac5d9c9f64d247004e"},{"id":"subset_new_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x^2=1\\}$ and $B=\\{-1,0,1\\}$. Which statement is true?","options":["$B\\subseteq A$","$A\\subseteq B$","$A=B$","$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c5be7081f8ff91f3bc5620a"}]}}